EXT. SKATEPARK POOL - DAWN

A drained kidney-shaped pool, cracked at the lip. Spray paint flares across the walls. Grey light, wet concrete. A beat-up board lies on its side.

MIKA (16, bruised elbow, helmet strap hanging) sits on the deck above the deep end, phone pressed to her ear, a tangle of nerves.

MIKA
Dom? You there?

DOM (V.O.)
(over phone, groggy)
It's five in the morning.

MIKA
I know.

DOM (V.O.)
What's wrong?

MIKA
Nothing. I'm at the pool.

DOM (V.O.)
Mika. You said you wouldn't.

MIKA
I said I wouldn't drop in without you.

DOM (V.O.)
That's the same thing.

MIKA
It's not.

She peers over the lip. The bowl yawns below her, thirteen feet at the deepest. She sets the phone on speaker and lays it beside her.

DOM (V.O.)
(faint)
Wait for me. I'm getting my shoes.

MIKA
I'm just looking.

DOM (V.O.)
Liar.

She laughs, short. Pulls on the helmet. Buckles it. Picks up the board and places it on the lip, tail on the coping.

MIKA
Okay. Okay. Okay.

DOM (V.O.)
Mika! Don't!

She steps on. The board tips. She shrieks, drops in, rockets down the wall, shoots across the flat bottom, climbs the far wall. She flies. She whoops. She slams down on her hip and skids on her back to the middle, laughing and swearing.

The phone crackles on the deck.

DOM (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Mika? Mika! Are you alive?

She stares at the sky. Raises her phone arm, a thumb up toward no one.

MIKA
(shouting)
Alive!

DOM (V.O.)
Don't ever do that again.

MIKA
Again.
